// SL-333 R4 (conformance row K125, refused half 2 of 2): TWO READ-ONLY windows
// through an EXCLUSIVE-ONLY accessor, on ONE `var` root, are two EXCLUSIVE
// receiver borrows and conflict.
//
// This is the cell that says the receiver's mode is a second fact rather than a
// restatement of the window's. Both sigils here are `&`, both windows only
// read, and the ordinary design-188 pairwise test — which reads the SIGILS —
// calls two shared borrows compatible and is right to. What it cannot see is
// that each of these windows takes the whole of `b` exclusively, because the
// accessor's epilogue mutates `self`: the same shape as
// `setboth(&var b.at(0), &var b.at(1))`, reached through a pair of `&`.
//
// The control is one line up in `K125_receiver_access_is_a_second_fact.saw`: the
// identical expression through a SHARED-ELIGIBLE accessor compiles, on a `let`
// root at that. The MIXED pair — one of each, in both orders — is
// `mixed_window_modes_conflict.saw`.
//
// It is the ORDINARY pairwise root-charge test that reports this, with the
// receiver borrow's mode as its one input: there is no second rule for two
// exclusive-only windows, which is why the mixed pair and a window beside a
// plain `&root` are refused by the same code with the same words.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: exclusive access violation: the place `b.tally(…)` borrows `b` EXCLUSIVELY even though this window only reads
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: `Bag.tally` mutates `self` outside the place it lends (a write into receiver storage at line 34)
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: and `b` is accessed by reference a second time in the same call
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: a read-only RESULT is not a shared receiver BORROW

struct Bag { cells: [Int; 4], visits: Int }

extension Bag {
    public func tally(&var self, i: Int) borrows -> &var Int {
        if i < 0 || i >= 4 { panic("Bag.tally: index out of range") }
        lend self.cells[i]
        self.visits = self.visits + 1
    }
}

func pair(a: &Int, b: &Int) -> Int { a + b }

func main() {
    var b = Bag(cells: [10, 20, 30, 40], visits: 0)
    print("{pair(&b.tally(0), &b.tally(1))}")
}
